Switch Statements

    What:
        A switch statement tests a variable (or expression) against a list of
        constant values. Each constant is called a "case". When a match is found,
        the code associated with that case runs.

    Why:
        Cleaner and easier to read than a long chain of if‑else‑if when you are
        comparing the same variable against many possible values.

    Where:
        Menu‑driven programs, simple state machines, command interpreters,
        handling enum values.

    How:
        The switch expression is evaluated once. The result is compared with each
        case label. If a matching case is found, execution starts from that point
        and continues until a `break` statement or the end of the switch block.

    ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

    Syntax:

        switch (expression) {
            case constant1:
                // code
                break;
            case constant2:
                // code
                break;
            // ... more cases ...
            default:
                // code (if no case matches)
        }

    ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

    Important Rules:

        • The expression must evaluate to an integer (char, short, int, long,
          long long, or enum). Floating‑point numbers and strings are not allowed.
        • Each case label must be a compile‑time constant (no variables).
        • `break` exits the switch block. Without it, execution "falls through" to
          the next case.
        • `default` is optional and runs when no other case matches.
        • You cannot have duplicate case values.

    ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

    How Fall‑Through Works:

        If you omit `break`, the program continues executing the next case's code.
        This is sometimes used intentionally, but can also be a bug.

        Example of intentional fall‑through:
            char ch;
            scanf(" %c", &ch);
            switch (ch) {
                case 'A':
                case 'a':
                    printf("Apple\n");
                    break;
                case 'B':
                case 'b':
                    printf("Banana\n");
                    break;
                default:
                    printf("Unknown\n");
            }

    ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

    Switch vs if‑else:

        Switch                                   If‑Else‑If
        ──────                                   ─────────
        Works only with integral constants.      Can use any condition (>, <, !=).
        Often more readable for exact equality.  Better for range checks.
        Compiler may optimise with a jump table.  Evaluated sequentially.
        Only one variable tested.                Can combine different variables.

    ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

    Nested Switch:

        You can place a switch inside another switch, but it should be used
        sparingly as it can become hard to read.

        Example:
            switch (mainChoice) {
                case 1:
                    switch (subChoice) {
                        case 'a': printf("1a\n"); break;
                        case 'b': printf("1b\n"); break;
                    }
                    break;
                case 2:
                    printf("Two\n");
                    break;
            }

    ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

    Common Pitfalls:

        • Forgetting `break` → unwanted fall‑through.
        • Using non‑integral expression (float, string) → compilation error.
        • Using non‑constant case labels (e.g., a variable) → error.
        • Not including a `default` case when unexpected values might occur.

    ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

Switch Statements – How the Compiler Implements Them

    When you write a switch statement, the C compiler does not simply execute it
    as a sequence of “if” checks. Depending on the case values, the compiler
    may use one of three internal strategies to make the switch run faster.

    These strategies are:
        1. If‑Else Chaining
        2. Binary Search
        3. Jump Table (Lookup Table)

    ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

    1. If‑Else Chaining
        • The compiler converts the switch into a simple chain of if‑else‑if
          statements.
        • Each case is checked in order, from top to bottom, until a match is
          found or the default is reached.
        • This is used when the number of cases is small, or the case values are
          very scattered (sparse).
        • Performance: O(n) – worst case when the matching case is at the end.

        Example:
            switch (num) {
                case 10:  ...; break;
                case 25:  ...; break;
                case 87:  ...; break;
                default:  ...;
            }
            // Internally becomes:
            if (num == 10) ...
            else if (num == 25) ...
            else if (num == 87) ...
            else ...

    ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

    2. Binary Search
        • When the case values are sorted but still sparse, the compiler may
          build a binary search tree.
        • It compares the expression with the middle case, then goes left or
          right, cutting the search space in half each time.
        • This is better than if‑else chaining for many cases, but still slower
          than a jump table.
        • Performance: O(log n).

        Example:
            switch (num) {
                case 5:  ...; break;
                case 12: ...; break;
                case 19: ...; break;
                case 33: ...; break;
                case 45: ...; break;
                default: ...;
            }
            // Might be implemented as:
            if (num < 19) {
                if (num < 12) { if (num == 5) ... else default }
                else { if (num == 12) ... else { if (num == 19) ... else default } }
            } else { ... }
            // (simplified illustration of the idea)

    ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

    3. Jump Table (Lookup Table)
        • The fastest method when the case values form a dense range (e.g., 0,
          1, 2, 3, 4, 5).
        • The compiler creates an array (table) of code addresses. The switch
          expression is used as an index directly into that table to jump to the
          correct case.
        • Performance: O(1) – constant time, regardless of the number of cases.
        • Requires cases to be consecutive or nearly consecutive (small gaps
          filled with default labels).

        Example:
            switch (cmd) {
                case 0: handle_exit(); break;
                case 1: handle_open(); break;
                case 2: handle_save(); break;
                case 3: handle_print(); break;
                default: handle_unknown();
            }
            // Compiler builds a table:
            jump_table[0] = address of case 0
            jump_table[1] = address of case 1
            jump_table[2] = address of case 2
            jump_table[3] = address of case 3
            // any other value goes to default
            // Execution: goto jump_table[cmd];

    ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

    How the Compiler Chooses:
        • It analyses the case values (their count, range, sparsity).
        • A jump table is preferred when the density is high (cases / range).
        • Binary search is used for moderately many cases that are not dense.
        • If‑else chaining is the fallback for very few or random cases.

    ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

    Key Takeaways:
        • You do not need to write these manually; the compiler does it for you.
        • Understanding these helps you know why a switch with consecutive
          integers is faster than one with random scattered numbers.
        • It also explains why cases must be compile‑time constants – they are
          used to build these tables/search trees at compile time.

    ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

Sample Questions – Switch Statements

    1. Simple Day of Week
       Problem : Read a number (1–7) and print the corresponding day of the week.
                (1 = Monday, 2 = Tuesday, …, 7 = Sunday).
       Solution:
            #include <stdio.h>
            int main() {
                int day;
                scanf("%d", &day);
                switch (day) {
                    case 1: printf("Monday\n");    break;
                    case 2: printf("Tuesday\n");   break;
                    case 3: printf("Wednesday\n"); break;
                    case 4: printf("Thursday\n");  break;
                    case 5: printf("Friday\n");    break;
                    case 6: printf("Saturday\n");  break;
                    case 7: printf("Sunday\n");    break;
                    default: printf("Invalid\n");
                }
                return 0;
            }

    2. Vowel or Consonant
       Problem : Read a character and print whether it is a vowel (a,e,i,o,u)
                or a consonant. Ignore case.
       Solution:
            #include <stdio.h>
            int main() {
                char ch;
                scanf(" %c", &ch);
                switch (ch) {
                    case 'A': case 'a':
                    case 'E': case 'e':
                    case 'I': case 'i':
                    case 'O': case 'o':
                    case 'U': case 'u':
                        printf("Vowel\n"); break;
                    default:
                        printf("Consonant\n");
                }
                return 0;
            }

    3. Simple Calculator
       Problem : Read two integers and an operator (+, -, *, /, %). Perform the
                corresponding operation and print the result.
       Solution:
            #include <stdio.h>
            int main() {
                int a, b;
                char op;
                scanf("%d %c %d", &a, &op, &b);
                switch (op) {
                    case '+': printf("%d\n", a + b); break;
                    case '-': printf("%d\n", a - b); break;
                    case '*': printf("%d\n", a * b); break;
                    case '/':
                        if (b != 0) printf("%d\n", a / b);
                        else printf("Division by zero\n");
                        break;
                    case '%':
                        if (b != 0) printf("%d\n", a % b);
                        else printf("Division by zero\n");
                        break;
                    default:  printf("Invalid operator\n");
                }
                return 0;
            }

