    7. Electricity Bill Calculator
       Problem : Write a program to calculate the electricity bill based on the
                following slab rates (units consumed per month):
                    0 – 100 units   : ₹ 1.50 per unit
                    101 – 200 units : ₹ 2.50 per unit
                    201 – 300 units : ₹ 4.00 per unit
                    above 300 units : ₹ 5.00 per unit
                The bill is calculated slab‑wise. Use a switch statement to
                select the appropriate slab for the number of units consumed.
                (Hint: map the units to a slab index, then compute the bill.)

       Solution (using switch with a computed slab index):
            #include <stdio.h>
            int main() {
                int units;
                float bill = 0.0;
                printf("Enter total units consumed: ");
                scanf("%d", &units);

                // Determine the slab index (0 to 3)
                // slab 0: 0-100, slab 1: 101-200, slab 2: 201-300, slab 3: >300
                int slab = (units <= 100) ? 0 :
                           (units <= 200) ? 1 :
                           (units <= 300) ? 2 : 3;

                switch (slab) {
                    case 3:
                        bill += (units - 300) * 5.00;
                        units = 300;
                        // no break — fall through
                    case 2:
                        bill += (units - 200) * 4.00;
                        units = 200;
                        // fall through
                    case 1:
                        bill += (units - 100) * 2.50;
                        units = 100;
                        // fall through
                    case 0:
                        bill += units * 1.50;
                        break;
                    default:
                        printf("Invalid units\n");
                        return 1;
                }

                printf("Total electricity bill = ₹ %.2f\n", bill);
                return 0;
            }

       Explanation:
            The switch uses fall‑through to accumulate the bill from the
            highest slab down to the lowest. After each slab, the remaining
            units are reduced to the upper bound of the previous slab.
            This technique avoids multiple if‑else blocks while correctly
            applying the progressive slab rates.
